# AshGraphLaw: admission as an Ash extension

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ash_graphlaw, path: __DIR__}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## The idea

AshGraphLaw is the Ash/BEAM membrane over the GraphLaw WASI engine (library 26.9.30, engine
release v26.9.28). An Ash action declares an *admission*; the engine derives and validates over
the projected RDF; the outcome is either evidence or a typed refusal.

```text
Ash action
    |
    v
graphlaw admission ---> projection (N-Triples) ---> GraphLaw engine (wasm, digest-pinned)
    |                                                    |
    v                                                    v
AshGraphLaw.Evidence  <--- admitted                 AshGraphLaw.Refusal (closed code table)
```

GraphLaw derives and validates. It never authorizes. Evidence is an observation bound to one
exact input digest, and a refusal is an observation about one exact input. Neither grants
authority.

This notebook installs the checkout you opened it from (`path: __DIR__`). Every output below is
produced when you evaluate the cell; none is pre-filled.

## Vendor the engine

The engine is a pinned artifact, not a dependency. Fetch it once, from a shell in this checkout;
it is kept only if its SHA-256 equals the pin in `priv/graphlaw/MANIFEST.json`:

```bash
mix ash_graphlaw.vendor
```

Then admit whatever is vendored, through the library. This cell never downloads anything. It
returns the engine identity when the bytes pass the import, export and digest gate, and a typed
refusal otherwise, including `:wasm_not_vendored` when step one has not been done.

```elixir
wasm_path = AshGraphLaw.WasmConfig.wasm_path([])

engine =
  case File.read(wasm_path) do
    {:ok, bytes} ->
      AshGraphLaw.EngineLoad.admit(bytes, [])

    {:error, reason} ->
      {:error,
       AshGraphLaw.Refusal.new(
         :wasm_not_vendored,
         "no engine at #{wasm_path} (#{inspect(reason)}); run mix ash_graphlaw.vendor"
       )}
  end

case engine do
  {:ok, admitted} -> {:engine_admitted, admitted.wasm_sha256}
  {:error, refusal} -> {:engine_refused, refusal.code, refusal.class, refusal.broken_term}
end
```

## Start the pool

The application starts no engine by default, so depending on the library never instantiates
wasm implicitly. Start the pool yourself (or set `config :ash_graphlaw, start_pool: true`). A
missing engine leaves the pool members supervised and unavailable; every call then answers with
a typed refusal.

```elixir
case AshGraphLaw.Pool.start_link(size: 1) do
  {:ok, _pool} -> :started
  {:error, {:already_started, _pool}} -> :already_running
end
```

Ask the engine what it supports. This is the first real round trip: an `{:ok, map}` when the
engine is vendored, a refusal when it is not.

```elixir
capabilities = AshGraphLaw.capabilities()

case capabilities do
  {:ok, response} -> {:capabilities, response["abi_version"], AshGraphLaw.abi_version()}
  {:error, refusal} -> {:refused, refusal.code, AshGraphLaw.Standing.of(capabilities)}
end
```

## Write a law module

A law module supplies the engine steps and, optionally, the exact graph the steps run over.
This one requires every ticket to carry a non-empty `xsd:string` title.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.TitleLaw do
  @behaviour AshGraphLaw.Law

  @ns "urn:example:demo:"
  @xsd_string "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"
  @rdf_type "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"

  @shapes """
  @prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
  @prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
  @prefix d: <#{@ns}> .

  d:TicketShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass d:Ticket ;
    sh:property [
      sh:path d:title ;
      sh:minCount 1 ;
      sh:datatype xsd:string ;
      sh:minLength 1
    ] .
  """

  @impl true
  def steps(_subject, _admission), do: [%{"step" => "shacl", "shapes" => @shapes}]

  @impl true
  def data(%Ash.Changeset{} = changeset, _admission) do
    id = changeset.data |> Map.get(:id) |> Kernel.||("new")
    subject = "<#{@ns}ticket:#{id}>"
    lines = ["#{subject} <#{@rdf_type}> <#{@ns}Ticket> .\n"]

    lines =
      case Ash.Changeset.get_attribute(changeset, :title) do
        nil -> lines
        title -> lines ++ ["#{subject} <#{@ns}title> #{literal(title)} .\n"]
      end

    {:ok, %{text: IO.iodata_to_binary(lines), dialect: "ntriples"}}
  end

  defp literal(text) do
    escaped =
      text
      |> String.replace("\\", "\\\\")
      |> String.replace("\"", "\\\"")
      |> String.replace("\n", "\\n")

    "\"#{escaped}\"^^<#{@xsd_string}>"
  end
end
```

## Define a resource with the graphlaw DSL

`:open` creates a ticket only if the `:title_shape` admission holds. `:seed` is ungated, so the
notebook can build a stored record. `:publish` needs the `:select` authority ceiling, which a
signed lease would grant; this notebook presents none, so it demonstrates the refusal.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Ticket do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshGraphLaw.Resource]

  ets do
    private? true
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :title, :string, public?: true
    attribute :state, :atom, default: :draft, public?: true, constraints: [one_of: [:draft, :published]]
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :open do
      accept [:title]
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :title_shape}
    end

    create :seed do
      accept [:title, :state]
    end

    update :publish do
      require_atomic? false
      change set_attribute(:state, :published)
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :publish_gate}
    end
  end

  graphlaw do
    runtime do
      timeout_ms(5000)
    end

    admission :title_shape do
      step(:shacl)
      law(Demo.TitleLaw)
    end

    admission :publish_gate do
      step(:shacl)
      ceiling(:select)
      law(Demo.TitleLaw)
    end
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource Demo.Ticket
  end
end
```

The DSL is introspectable without the engine. The compiled admissions are data:

```elixir
for admission <- AshGraphLaw.Admissions.all(Demo.Ticket) do
  {admission.name, admission.step, admission.ceiling}
end
```

## Admit

A helper runs an action and keeps the evidence the admission wrote into the changeset context.
Evidence is captured in an `after_action` hook, so it is only ever what the engine reported for
this exact input.

```elixir
create_with_evidence = fn params ->
  parent = self()

  result =
    Demo.Ticket
    |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:open, params)
    |> Ash.Changeset.after_action(fn changeset, record ->
      send(parent, {:evidence, changeset.context[:graphlaw]})
      {:ok, record}
    end)
    |> Ash.create()

  evidence =
    receive do
      {:evidence, evidence} -> evidence
    after
      0 -> nil
    end

  {result, evidence}
end

{admitted_result, evidence} = create_with_evidence.(%{title: "Fix the build"})

case admitted_result do
  {:ok, ticket} -> {:admitted, ticket.title, evidence.standing}
  {:error, error} -> {:refused, AshGraphLaw.Error.codes(error)}
end
```

With the engine vendored the ticket is created and its standing is `:PARTIAL_ALIVE`: the
admission was observed on this exact input and no consequence was executed by the engine.
Without the engine the same call is refused with a typed code and nothing is persisted.

## Refuse

An empty title violates the shape (`sh:minLength 1`), so the engine refuses. Every refusal is a
closed, typed value. Read its code, class and Chatman broken term:

```elixir
{refused_result, _no_evidence} = create_with_evidence.(%{title: ""})

case refused_result do
  {:ok, _ticket} ->
    {:unexpected, :admitted}

  {:error, error} ->
    for refusal <- AshGraphLaw.Error.refusals(error) do
      {refusal.code, refusal.class, refusal.broken_term, AshGraphLaw.Standing.of({:error, refusal})}
    end
end
```

## Refuse on authority

`:publish_gate` requires ceiling `:select`. No lease is presented, so the claim is `:observe` and
the change refuses with `:ceiling_unmet` before the engine is called. This refusal is decided in
Elixir and is identical whether or not the engine is vendored.

```elixir
seeded = Ash.create!(Demo.Ticket, %{title: "Ship it"}, action: :seed)

publish_result =
  seeded
  |> Ash.Changeset.for_update(:publish, %{})
  |> Ash.update()

case publish_result do
  {:ok, _} ->
    {:unexpected, :published}

  {:error, error} ->
    [refusal] = AshGraphLaw.Error.refusals(error)
    {refusal.code, refusal.class, refusal.broken_term, refusal.details}
end
```

## Read Evidence and Standing

Evidence is data bound to a digest. Its `digest` is recomputable from its own content, and it
names the input, the admission and the engine that answered.

```elixir
case evidence do
  %AshGraphLaw.Evidence{} = ev ->
    %{
      admission: ev.admission,
      standing: ev.standing,
      describe: AshGraphLaw.Standing.describe(ev.standing),
      input_digest: ev.input_digest,
      wasm_sha256: ev.wasm_sha256,
      graphlaw_release: ev.graphlaw_release,
      digest_recomputes?: ev.digest == AshGraphLaw.Evidence.digest(ev)
    }

  nil ->
    {:no_evidence, "the engine did not admit, so there is nothing to bind a digest to"}
end
```

The standing vocabulary is closed and `:ALIVE` is never produced by an admission alone.

```elixir
Enum.map(AshGraphLaw.Standing.all(), fn standing -> {standing, AshGraphLaw.Standing.describe(standing)} end)
```

## Typed capabilities

Every operation of the engine has a typed function, so no supported op needs `AshGraphLaw.call/2`.
The modules are generated from the GraphLaw capability registry; this notebook only calls them.
Each cell prints what the call returned for this run: a result struct name when the engine is
vendored, or a typed refusal code when it is not. A result is an observation, at most
`PARTIAL_ALIVE`, and grants nothing.

```elixir
outcome = fn
  {:ok, %module{}} -> {:ok, module |> Module.split() |> List.last()}
  {:error, %AshGraphLaw.Refusal{code: code}} -> {:refused, code}
end

registry = AshGraphLaw.Capability.Registry

%{
  schema: registry.schema(),
  ops: registry.names(),
  registry_digest: registry.digest(),
  surface_digest: registry.surface_digest()
}
```

Routing and syntax: `sniff`, `parse`, `convert` and `canonical`.

```elixir
ttl = "@prefix ex: <https://e/> . ex:s ex:p ex:o, 42 ; a ex:T ."

%{
  sniff: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.sniff(text: ttl)),
  parse: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.parse(text: ttl, dialect: "turtle")),
  convert: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.convert(text: ttl, dialect: "turtle", to: "ntriples")),
  canonical: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.canonical(data: ttl))
}
```

Queries and validation: `sparql` (three result kinds), `shacl` and `shex`. The engine evaluates
them; AshGraphLaw builds the request and decodes the answer.

```elixir
data = "@prefix ex: <https://e/> . ex:s a ex:T ; ex:p 1 ."

shapes = """
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> . @prefix ex: <https://e/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
ex:S a sh:NodeShape ; sh:targetClass ex:T ;
  sh:property [ sh:path ex:p ; sh:datatype xsd:integer ] .
"""

%{
  select: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.sparql(data: data, query: "SELECT ?o WHERE { <https://e/s> <https://e/p> ?o }")),
  construct: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.sparql(data: data, query: "CONSTRUCT { ?s a <https://e/U> } WHERE { ?s a <https://e/T> }")),
  ask: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.sparql(data: data, query: "ASK { <https://e/s> a <https://e/T> }")),
  shacl: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.shacl(data: data, shapes: shapes)),
  shex:
    outcome.(
      AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.shex(
        data: "@prefix ex: <https://e/> . ex:cat ex:says \"meow\" .",
        schema: "PREFIX ex: <https://e/>\nex:Cat { ex:says . }",
        map: "<https://e/cat>@<https://e/Cat>"
      )
    )
}
```

Derivation: `n3`, `entail` and `datalog`.

```elixir
%{
  n3:
    outcome.(
      AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.n3(
        text: "@prefix : <https://e/> . :s a :Human . { ?x a :Human } => { ?x a :Mortal } ."
      )
    ),
  entail:
    outcome.(
      AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.entail(
        data: "@prefix ex: <https://e/> . @prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> . ex:Cat rdfs:subClassOf ex:Animal . ex:tom a ex:Cat .",
        regime: "rdfs"
      )
    ),
  datalog:
    outcome.(
      AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.datalog(
        facts: [["https://e/a", "https://e/par", "https://e/b"]],
        rules: [%{"head" => ["?x", "https://e/anc", "?y"], "body" => [["?x", "https://e/par", "?y"]]}]
      )
    )
}
```

Composition: `hooks`, `law` and `policy`. `law` decodes to `AshGraphLaw.Admitted`.

```elixir
pack = """
@prefix kh: <http://seanchatmangpt.github.io/praxis/kh#> . @prefix ex: <https://e/> .
ex:h a kh:Hook ; kh:kind "sparql" ; kh:effect "emit-delta" ; kh:action ex:a ;
  kh:query "SELECT ?x WHERE { ?x a <https://e/A> }" .
ex:a a kh:Action ; kh:handler <http://seanchatmangpt.github.io/praxis/handler#sparql-construct> ;
  kh:query "CONSTRUCT { ?x a <https://e/B> } WHERE { ?x a <https://e/A> }" .
"""

problem = ~s({"states": [{"id": "s0"}, {"id": "g", "facts": ["done"]}], "initial_states": ["s0"], "goal": {"facts": ["done"]}, "transitions": [{"action": "flip", "from": "s0", "to": "g", "probability_ppm": 500000}, {"action": "flip", "from": "s0", "to": "s0", "probability_ppm": 500000}]})
policy = ~s({"policy": [{"state": "s0", "action": "flip", "outcomes": [{"state": "g", "probability_ppm": 500000}, {"state": "s0", "probability_ppm": 500000}]}]})

%{
  hooks: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.hooks(pack: pack, data: "@prefix ex: <https://e/> . ex:x a ex:A .")),
  law:
    outcome.(
      AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.law(
        data: %{"text" => "<urn:a:C> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <urn:a:D> .\n<urn:a:x> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <urn:a:C> .\n", "dialect" => "ntriples"},
        steps: [%{"step" => "rdfs"}]
      )
    ),
  policy: outcome.(AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.policy(problem: problem, policy: policy))
}
```

A malformed request is refused by the client before any engine call, with the reason in
`details`. This cell gives the same answer with or without the engine.

```elixir
{:error, refusal} = AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.shacl(data: "@prefix ex: <https://e/> . ex:s a ex:T .")

{refusal.code, refusal.class, refusal.details["missing"]}
```

Decoding is forward-compatible: `from_map/1` never raises, and `raw` keeps the whole response,
including keys this version has no field for.

```elixir
response = %{"ok" => true, "id" => "sha256:0", "nquads" => "", "quads" => 0, "a_newer_field" => 1}
decoded = AshGraphLaw.Result.Canonical.from_map(response)

%{lossless?: decoded.raw == response, newer_field: decoded.raw["a_newer_field"], id: decoded.id}
```

Every capability call emits a telemetry span. Attach a handler, make one call, and read what the
span reported.

```elixir
parent = self()
handler_id = "livebook-capability-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

:telemetry.attach(
  handler_id,
  [:ash_graphlaw, :capability, :stop],
  fn _event, _measurements, metadata, _config -> send(parent, {:span, metadata.op, metadata.outcome, metadata.refusal_code}) end,
  nil
)

_ = AshGraphLaw.Capability.API.sniff(text: "SELECT * WHERE { ?s ?p ?o }")
:telemetry.detach(handler_id)

receive do
  {:span, op, outcome, code} -> {op, outcome, code}
after
  1_000 -> :no_span_received
end
```

The parity court compares the typed surface with the engine's `capabilities` answer. The engine pin
(`v26.9.28`) predates the registry (`26.9.29`), so a drift verdict is a valid outcome here and is
reported as it is. This cell skips the op-example replay (`examples: false`); run
`mix ash_graphlaw.parity` for the full court.

```elixir
case AshGraphLaw.Parity.run(examples: false) do
  {:ok, report} ->
    {:parity_holds, for(check <- report["checks"], do: {check["id"], check["status"]})}

  {:error, %{refusal: refusal, report: report}} ->
    {:parity_refused, refusal.code, for(check <- report["checks"], do: {check["id"], check["status"]})}
end
```

